WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:00.760
 Hi.

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:01.260
Hi!

00:00:05.257 --> 00:00:06.840
When you play something
like The Sims,

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.048
the player is actually the
one building the rule set.

00:00:09.048 --> 00:00:11.040
I'm giving the player
a toy, and the player

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.650
is turning it into a game.

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:13.394
SIM: Wee-- ah!

00:00:13.394 --> 00:00:14.310
 It's almost magical.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:20.620
As a designer, you're actually
dealing with two computers.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:22.060
First, the electronic
one sitting

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:24.820
on the table in front of
you, but more importantly,

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:29.150
the player's imagination,
the players brain.

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:31.681
Being a game designer turned
into a lifelong learning

00:00:31.681 --> 00:00:33.680
process where I can go
off in any subject I want

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:37.652
to and it's tax deductible.

00:00:37.652 --> 00:00:40.110
I'm going to expose you to a
number of fundamental concepts

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:41.130
about game design.

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:42.880
I want to dive deep
into the psychology

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.240
of the player, the mental
modeling that goes on,

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.700
how to build
emergence, surprise,

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.250
and to try to predict
what's going to motivate

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:51.810
players and pull them in.

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:53.520
You can take almost
anything and make it

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.870
a fascinating,
interactive experience.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:58.370
When you watch
somebody play a game,

00:00:58.370 --> 00:00:59.953
most of what they're
doing is failing.

00:00:59.953 --> 00:01:02.000
You need to design the
game such that failure is

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.500
interesting and understandable,
so that you go back and say,

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.720
aha!

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:07.799
 Oh, geez.

00:01:07.799 --> 00:01:09.090
 This is actually kind of fun.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:11.960
 Good.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:14.910
 Games and film have a very
different emotional palette.

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:17.120
Film is totally
based upon empathy.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:20.330
You can look at characters, when
they're happy, we feel happy.

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:23.600
The kind of emotions that we
can actually get out of games

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.300
are very different.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:29.000
Things like guilt,
or pride, teamwork.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:30.702
A lot of that is
based upon agency.

00:01:30.702 --> 00:01:32.660
As soon as you take that
away from the players,

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:34.076
it's kind of like
being in a movie

00:01:34.076 --> 00:01:36.760
theater where all of a
sudden the screen goes black.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.259
Well that's what it's like when
you take the control away

00:01:39.259 --> 00:01:40.241
from the player.

00:01:40.241 --> 00:01:41.740
The generation
that's creating games

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:43.750
now has so many opportunities.

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:45.250
You're having a
vision for something

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:46.960
that's going to go
out and change people.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:48.790
So find something
you're passionate about

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:50.580
and start exploring.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:53.710
I'm Will Wright, and this is
a simulation-- a simulation--

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:54.880
a simulation--

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:56.710
of my MasterClass.

